alk of revenge in russia too at the  memorial for nationalist tv commentator  daria dugina ukraine denies russian  claims it was behind the car bomb that  killed her on saturday  her father far-right ideologue alexander  dugan says she died in the name of  russian truth and  russian victory  at an international diplomatic  conference in kiev zielinski told world  leaders that his country's victory must  include taking back crimea annexed by  russia eight years ago we can't forget  that putin's invasion of ukraine really  started in 2014. we should have the  humility to acknowledge that not  everybody realized the sheer enormity of  what was happening at the time the west  may have regrets about crimea but it  can't afford regrets about zaparisia the  head of the international atomic energy  agency says he expects a mission to  finally be allowed to visit the  russian-controlled nuclear power plant  within days  both sides in the war blame the other  for the ongoing shelling being reported  there risking catastrophe at europe's  largest nuclear plant the city beside it  is named ener hodar energy's gift  of course we are worried how can it not  worry us says this man  it's like sitting on a powder keg  redmond shannon global news london  all dried up ahead the stark toll of  drought conditions plaguing earth's  northern  hemisphere over the past few months  we've shared stark images like these  highlighting the severity of droughts  and water shortages impacting much of  the northern hemisphere water bodies  like lakes rivers and canals are rapidly  receding or worse drying up creating  water shortages as eric sorensen reports  while the immediate conditions are  causing worry in many countries the  future impact is alarming  in china there's been so little rain  that a 600 year old statue of buddha has  been uncovered on a dried-up stretch of  the yangtze river satellite pictures  show chinese rivers full in april and  parched in august  news broadcasts show fire trucks  delivering emergency water everywhere  crops are withering  china's drought is remarkable but what's  extraordinary is how many intense dry  spells are happening around the world  the worst conditions are in east africa  the u.n says as many as 22 million  people could face extreme hunger in the  months ahead  there was no rain he says whatever we  planted did not grow one factor is the  la nina current persisting for a third  year in the eastern pacific it  contributes to dry conditions as far  away as the horn of africa but also  across the pacific from chile to mexico  to the southwestern u.s all enduring  exceptionally dry weather it's a cause  of concern because of all the  climate impacts that la nina has in the  form of drought climate change is  amplifying the impacts of these la ninas  even though it may not be having a  direct impact on  the occurrence of la nina itself while  canada has been relatively spared this  summer drought is scorching much of the  northern hemisphere virtually no  european country has escaped extreme and  persistent dry conditions  rivers all over western europe have  dropped to record low levels  in serbia scuttled german ships from the  second world war have been uncovered  while in england several kilometers of  the thames river is bone dry drinking  water for some is being delivered if you  don't have running water it makes you  question all the most basic sort of  complexities of life but in eastern  africa any water would be welcome  conflict on top of drought has made the  situation dire the driest season that we  have seen  in decades four or five rainy seasons  have just disappeared the world food  program says in this part of the world  people cannot wait for the weather to  change facing starvation they need food  and water now  eric sorensen global news toronto  next the world renowned architect  gifting his modern wonders to the next  generation  one of the world's most celebrated  architects moshe safdi is the 